  "summary": "An architectural style incorporating elements from the architecture of Ancient Egypt.",
  "description": "Characterized by massive forms, battered walls, papyrus columns, and lotus motifs, it emphasized mystery and monumentality. This style emerged following Napoleon's campaign in Egypt and the subsequent publication of Description de l'Égypte, which sparked European fascination with Egyptian antiquity.",
  "culturalContext": "Egyptian Revival — European and American architecture borrowing pyramids, pylons, lotus columns and hieroglyphic friezes — surged after Napoleon's Description de l'Égypte (1809-29), Champollion's decipherment (1822) and the obelisk trade: cemetery gates (Highgate's Egyptian Avenue), synagogues (the \"Egyptian\" style read as Temple-of-Solomon), cinemas (Egyptian and Grauman's theatres), and monuments like the Washington Monument's obelisk form. It peaked as architectural exoticism for banks, prisons (the \"Egyptian\" prison type) and picture palaces.",
